  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nGlobal models are coming into somewhat better agreement with the \npattern evolution this weekend. Both the ECMWF and GFS show high \npressure strengthening over the southern Plains with the western \nperiphery of the ridge extending over E AZ and NM. This will cause\nthe subtropical jet to shift wwd beginning Saturday, setting up \nbetter moisture advection over southeast CA and southwest AZ. NBM \nPoPs are still not overly impressive, mainly in the 20-30% range \non Saturday, however the GEFS/GFS indicates a final slug of \nmoisture from Marie being pulled into southeast CA on Sunday with \nPWATs rising above 2.0\". Therefore we will have to monitor for \nheavier rainfall potential in portions of SE California including \nJoshua Tree NP on Sunday. Elsewhere, across southcentral AZ, rain \nchances look to be meager due to more extensive cloud cover and \nvery low instability. The latest WPC QPF field shows a local \nminimum in rainfall totals (<0.10\") in southcentral AZ including \nthe Phoenix Metro through this weekend. By early next week, there \nare large discrepancies in the ridge/trough positioning which will\ncomplicate the precipitation forecast, but there will still be \nadequate moisture across our region which will keep low end PoPs \nin place across eastern and southeastern AZ Monday and Tuesday \nwith potentially better precipitation chances arriving Wednesday.\n\nThe temperature forecast this weekend into early next week remains \nlargely unchanged, although there will likely be a gradual increase \nfrom near normal temperatures on Saturday to slightly above normal \ntemperatures Sunday into early next week as ridging strengthens over \nthe region. Depending on how strong the ridge gets, NBM temps could \nclimb a few more degrees in subsequent runs and it is not out of\nthe question that we could see lower desert highs approaching 110\ndegrees as indicated by the NBM 75th percentile on Monday and Tuesday.",
